y don't have those. T6 could be part of a chow with T4 and T5, but again, no. So T2 and T6 are single.\n\n- W5 and W6: maybe part of a chow with W4 or W7. But they don't have those. So W5 and W6 are single.\n\n- F3 and F4: could be part of a chow if they have F2 or F5, but they don't. So they are single.\n\n- J1 and J2: single.\n\nThis is looking messy. Maybe the hand is not structured for all chows. Let's check if there are any pungs. They have two B1s, but not three. Other tiles are single. So no pungs.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is aiming for a Seven Pairs (24 points). Let's see. For seven pairs, they need seven pairs. The current hand has B1 (2), and the rest are single. To make seven pairs, they need to have pairs in other tiles. For example, if they have B1, B1 as a pair, then they need five more pairs. But they have W5, W6, T2, T6, B3, B4, B5, B8, F3, F4, J1, J2. Each of these is a single. So unless they discard one tile to make another pair, but they have 13 tiles. Wait, no, they have 13 tiles. To form seven pairs, they need 14 tiles. Wait, no. Seven pairs require 14 tiles. But they have 13, so they need to draw one more. But since they are discarding, maybe they are trying to set up for seven pairs by discarding a tile to make six pairs and then draw the 14th. Let's see:\n\nCurrent hand has B1 (2), which is one pair. The rest are single. If they discard a tile, say B3, then they have B1 (2), and the rest are single. Then they need to draw a tile to make another pair. But that seems unlikely. Alternatively, maybe they have other pairs. For example, J1 and J2 are both present once. If they discard one of them, they can have a pair. But they need seven pairs. Let's see:\n\nIf they discard J1, then they have J2 as a single. If they can form pairs in other tiles. For example, if they have B1 (2), and maybe F3 and F4 could be pairs if they have another. But no, they only have one each. So this seems difficult. Maybe seven pairs isn't the way to go here.\n\n