7 form a Chow. T2 and T6 are single tiles. B1, B4, B5 could form another Chow (B1, B2, B3 is already a meld), but wait, B1 is already in the Chow. Wait, after forming B1, B2, B3, the remaining B1 is a single tile. The B4 and B5 are single. So maybe not helpful. Alternatively, maybe the T2 and T6 can form a pair? No, they are different. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe there's a way to form other melds. But with the current tiles, after forming the B1-2-3 Chow, the remaining B1 is a single, and B4 and B5 are single. Not helpful. Maybe this isn't the best move.\\n\\nNow, Chi B3. If the current discard is B3, then I can form B2, B3, B4. But do I have B2 and B4? I have B4, but not B2. Unless the current discard is B3 and I have B2 and B4. But I don't have B2. Wait, unless the current discard is B3, and I have B2 from my hand. But I don't have B2. So this seems impossible. Therefore, the valid actions might be a mistake. But the problem states that Chi B3 is valid, so perhaps there's a B3 discard available. Maybe the previous player discarded B3? Let me check again. The previous player's game history is Play B2. No B3. The player across's history is Play T3. The next player's history is Play B6. So where is B3 coming from? Maybe the current discard is B3 from the player across? Let me check their melds. They have a Chi of B5, B6, B7. So they took a B6 discard. But their own plays include T3. So if they discarded B3, but that's not in their history. So perhaps the current discard is B3 from the previous player? But they played B2. So this is confusing. Maybe the problem has a typo, and the valid actions are only Chi B2. Assuming that, then the best move is to Chi B2 to form the Chow. But after that, the remaining tiles may not form a good hand. Alternatively, maybe not taking any Chi and passing to see if I can draw a tile to complete a meld.\\n\\nAnother angle: my current hand has W5, W6, W7 (a Chow), T2 and T6 (could be part of a Chow if I get T3 or T5?), B1x2, B3, B4, B5. If 