orth, maybe not. If I discard F3, which is West Wind, since the seat wind is East, but the prevailing is North. Not sure. Wait, F1 is East, F2 is South, F3 is West, F4 is North. The current seat wind is East, so if I have a Pung of F1 (East), that would give Seat Wind points. But since I don't have multiple F3s, maybe discarding F3 is safe.\n\nLooking at the B4, B5, B6. They are each single tiles. If I discard B5, there are 3 left. If I discard B6, there are 2 left. So B6 is more scarce. So maybe it's better to discard B5 or B4 instead.\n\nThe W5, W6, W7 are in sequence. If I discard one of them, I might break the sequence. But if I keep them, they can form a Chow. So maybe it's better not to discard any of them. So W5, W6, W7 are important for a possible Chow. So if I have to discard one, maybe choose a tile from another suit.\n\nThe T6 and T9 are single tiles. T9 has 4 left in the wall. T6 has 4. So discarding either might be safe.\n\nThe F3 and F4 are in the hand. Since I have only one each, and the unshown counts for F3 is 3 and F4 is 3, maybe discarding one is safe.\n\nSo possible options are to play F3, F4, T6, T9, B4, B5, B6.\n\nBetween these, which is the best to discard. Considering that the melds are in B1-B3, and the B4-B6 are single, maybe discarding B4 is better because there are 4 left, which is more than B5 (3) and B6 (2). So B4 is more available. So playing B4 might be the safest.\n\nAlternatively, F3 or F4. If I discard F3, there are 3 left. F4 has 3 as well. But since the seat wind is East (F1), and the prevailing is North (F4), if I discard F4, maybe another player could use it for a Pung if they have two. But since the player across has a meld of B5-B6-B7, and the previous player has a Kong of J1, maybe not. The next player has played F1, F4, etc. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, the T6 and T9 are single. Since T9 has 4 left, maybe safer to discard T9. But if I discard T6, there are 4 left. Either is okay.\n\nSo among the options, maybe the best is to