cross has B5-B6-B7, so they have B6 in their meld. If I discard T6, maybe they can't use it. The next player has no melds, so maybe they can't Chi. The previous player has a Pung of J1, so not relevant. So T6 is safe.\n\nDiscarding T9 is a terminal; others might take it for a Pung or Chi. For example, if someone has T7 and T8, they could Chi T9. But looking at the game history, the next player has played T2, T7, etc. Not sure.\n\nDiscarding W1: a terminal. Others might take it for a Pung or Chi. For example, if someone has W2 and W3, they could Chi W1. But since W1 is a terminal, maybe less likely.\n\nBetween T6, T9, and W1, discarding T6 seems the safest. So the answer would be Play T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding T6 is the safest option as it is not part of any potential future melds and avoids giving others a chance to form Chows.]\nReason: [T6 is not part of any existing or potential Chows in my hand. Discarding it preserves key tiles like B4, B5, B6 for forming a Dots Chi and W5, W6, W7 for a Characters Chi. Other options like T9 or W1 are terminals that might be targeted by opponents.]\nAnswer: Play T6"}
